WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.436
The Competency Standards name two red flag detections we must check at once: ransomware, and PE infection,

2
00:00:07.436 --> 00:00:10.357
which is a virus that infects program files.

3
00:00:10.357 --> 00:00:22.240
In this demonstration I read endpoint file events from three school computers and decide which shows ransomware, which shows a PE infection, and which is normal approved software.

4
00:00:23.339 --> 00:00:25.157
First, a summary.

5
00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:31.571
cut keeps column two, the host, and column five, the action.

6
00:00:31.571 --> 00:00:35.527
sort and uniq dash c count each pair.

7
00:00:35.527 --> 00:00:40.338
LIB-PC-02 renamed five files and created one.

8
00:00:40.338 --> 00:00:45.897
MKT-PC-04 modified three files and had a quarantine.

9
00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:49.211
REG-PC-01 modified three files.

10
00:00:50.311 --> 00:00:53.558
Let's look at the renames on LIB-PC-02.

11
00:00:53.558 --> 00:00:59.470
Fields one, three, six and seven: time, process, old name and new name.

12
00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:06.297
Every file got the same new extension, dot crypt nine, within twenty-four seconds,

13
00:01:06.297 --> 00:01:11.791
done by winhelper dot exe running from the AppData Roaming folder.

14
00:01:12.891 --> 00:01:17.393
grep dash c counts the matching lines: five files.

15
00:01:17.393 --> 00:01:28.106
And the next event, the one that created HOW TO GET FILES BACK dot txt on the desktop, is the ransom note, red flag R2.

16
00:01:28.106 --> 00:01:31.257
LIB-PC-02 is a ransomware red flag.

17
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.147
Escalate immediately.

18
00:01:34.247 --> 00:01:36.108
Now the other two hosts.

19
00:01:36.108 --> 00:01:42.389
Both modified files, so I ask: which program did it, and who signed that program?

20
00:01:42.389 --> 00:01:50.375
On Windows, Import-Csv and Group-Object signer group the events by the company that signed the process.

21
00:01:51.475 --> 00:02:02.141
Unsigned processes made nine changes: the five renames, the ransom note, and three modified executables on MKT-PC-04.

22
00:02:02.141 --> 00:02:05.514
Microsoft Windows signed four events.

23
00:02:05.514 --> 00:02:12.169
One is the antivirus engine, and three are TiWorker dot exe on REG-PC-01.

24
00:02:12.169 --> 00:02:18.094
TiWorker is Windows Update, which replaces DLL files in System32.

25
00:02:18.094 --> 00:02:24.019
It is on the SOP's approved list, so REG-PC-01 is not a red flag.

26
00:02:25.119 --> 00:02:27.636
Last, the antivirus detection.

27
00:02:27.636 --> 00:02:32.838
Where-Object keeps the rows whose detection starts with Virus.

28
00:02:32.838 --> 00:02:41.479
On MKT-PC-04, a program called keygen dot exe modified three installers in the shared Marketing folder,

29
00:02:41.479 --> 00:02:46.346
and then Defender found Virus Win32 Sality in one of them.

30
00:02:46.346 --> 00:02:53.309
Executables modified by a non-updater, plus a Virus detection: red flags V1 and V2.

31
00:02:53.309 --> 00:02:54.567
A PE infection.

32
00:02:55.667 --> 00:02:57.736
Here is my red flag check.

33
00:02:57.736 --> 00:03:04.021
Two red flags, both escalated immediately, with the evidence and the SOP codes.

34
00:03:04.021 --> 00:03:08.556
One normal host, ruled out by its process and its signer.

35
00:03:08.556 --> 00:03:17.387
That last step matters: if we escalated every Windows Update, the Shift Lead would stop trusting our red flags.

36
00:03:18.487 --> 00:03:19.355
Your turn.

37
00:03:19.355 --> 00:03:22.565
In the CTM Virtual Lab, choose Day 5.

38
00:03:22.565 --> 00:03:29.333
In Activity 5.3, check the file events of five hosts with these same commands.

39
00:03:29.333 --> 00:03:34.365
Then, in Activity 5.4, check your own WKS-LAB workstation.

40
00:03:34.365 --> 00:03:41.479
Every seat has different evidence, so trust your own screen, not your classmate's.

